<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne Robert Jacques Turgot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1727">1727</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Turgot, Baron de l'Aulne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne Robert Jacques Turgot, Baron de l'Aulne, was a French economist and statesman, sometimes considered a physiocrat and best remembered as an early advocate of economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>. As intendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> in 1761–1774 he sought a fairer assessment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a> and replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> with a money tax, and in 1766 he wrote his Reflections on the Formation and Distribution of Wealth. Appointed Controller-General of Finance by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> in 1774, he freed the grain trade and in 1776 presented the Six Edicts, which suppressed the corvées and the privileges of the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. Opposition at court forced his resignation in May 1776, and he spent his last years in scientific and literary study.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anne-robert-jacques-turgot.jpg" alt="Anne Robert Jacques Turgot" width="330" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François-Hubert Drouais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_de_Turgot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Turgot was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 10 May 1727, the youngest son of Michel-Étienne Turgot, provost of the merchants of Paris, from an old Norman family. He was educated for the Church and admitted to the Sorbonne in 1749, but in 1750 decided not to take holy orders, saying that he could not bear to wear a mask all his life. That year he wrote a work on the Idea of Progress, which covers the whole of culture, including institutions, legal codes and economy. He met the physiocrats Quesnay and Vincent de Gournay and wrote articles for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1761 Turgot became intendant of the generalité of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, among the poorest and most over-taxed parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and remained thirteen years. He surveyed the land to assess the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a> more justly and replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> with a money tax, which let him leave a good system of highways.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> made him minister of the navy in July 1774 and Controller-General of Finances on 24 August. His principles were no bankruptcy, no increase of taxation and no borrowing. His edict of 13 September 1774 freed the grain trade, and after the poor harvest of 1774 he firmly repressed the bread riots known as the guerre des farines. In January 1776 he presented the Six Edicts, of which the two most opposed suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvées</a> and the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>' privileges. He had nearly everyone against him, the queen included, and on 12 May 1776 he was ordered to resign. He spent his last years in scientific and literary study and died on 18 March 1781.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known work, Reflections on the Formation and Distribution of Wealth, was written in 1766 and published separately in 1776. It divides society into three classes, develops Quesnay's theory that land is the only source of wealth, proposes a single tax on the land's net product and demands complete freedom of commerce and industry. He is thought to have been the first political economist to postulate something like the law of diminishing marginal returns in agriculture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anne Robert Jacques Turgot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1776 the minister <b>Turgot</b> abolished the guilds’ privileges, but they were soon restored after his fall that same year.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1762" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anne Robert Jacques Turgot"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1762">What was here in 1762? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
